WEBVTT

00:00:04.600 --> 00:00:05.796
Embeddings.

00:00:06.116 --> 00:00:07.833
Every word gets coordinates.

00:00:08.433 --> 00:00:13.085
Pylon showed you that a word in a model is a list of numbers, a place.

00:00:13.405 --> 00:00:15.818
Tonight, where those places come from.

00:00:17.018 --> 00:00:21.431
At the start, every word gets a list of 32 small random numbers.

00:00:21.751 --> 00:00:23.556
The places mean nothing yet.

00:00:24.156 --> 00:00:31.438
Check the neighbours: only 26 percent of words sit next to a word of the same kind, about what a shuffle gives.

00:00:32.638 --> 00:00:34.399
The trick is a guessing game.

00:00:34.719 --> 00:00:37.415
Take a word in a sentence, and the words around it.

00:00:38.015 --> 00:00:43.580
Each word gets two lists: one for when it's the word in the middle, one for when it's a neighbour.

00:00:43.900 --> 00:00:52.791
Nudge the lists so that the middle word's list and each real neighbour's neighbour-list get a bigger dot product, the measure from the vectors lesson.

00:00:53.391 --> 00:01:00.108
And for every real neighbour, pick 5 words at random, common words more often than rare ones, and push those the other way.

00:01:00.428 --> 00:01:06.559
Tomas Mikolov and his colleagues called it negative sampling, in 2013.

00:01:07.759 --> 00:01:13.237
Run the game over the guides' sentences, 5 times through, and watch the places move.

00:01:13.837 --> 00:01:17.033
Will the neighbours keep getting tidier all the way through training?

00:01:19.533 --> 00:01:27.902
Very early, by 5 percent of the way, the neighbours look their tidiest of any checkpoint: 72 percent of words sit next to a word of the same kind.

00:01:28.222 --> 00:01:38.374
By the end, in this run, it has slipped back to 65, three words fewer out of 46, while the guessing game keeps getting better on sentences it never trained on.

00:01:38.974 --> 00:01:41.801
Better at the game is not always tidier on the chart.

00:01:42.121 --> 00:01:45.534
The game is the goal; the neat neighbours are a side effect.

00:01:46.734 --> 00:01:58.081
Start from different random numbers, four more times, and you get different coordinates, but much the same neighbours: against the first run, 75 to 80 percent of each word's three nearest neighbours agree.

00:01:59.281 --> 00:02:08.455
The language model from the attention lesson learned its word lists a different way: as a side effect of guessing the next word, through two layers of attention.

00:02:09.055 --> 00:02:12.947
Its lists put 80 percent of words next to a word of the same kind.

00:02:13.267 --> 00:02:18.353
And between the two tables, about 33 percent of each word's three nearest neighbours agree.

00:02:18.673 --> 00:02:20.999
By chance it would be about 7 percent.

00:02:21.599 --> 00:02:28.469
So the two methods agree on far more than chance, and far less than two runs of the same method agree with each other.

00:02:28.789 --> 00:02:30.746
Neither one is the true map.

00:02:31.066 --> 00:02:34.152
Each is a map of what its own game needed.

00:02:35.352 --> 00:02:36.961
Nobody draws the chart.

00:02:37.281 --> 00:02:41.173
A guessing game moves the cards, and words used alike end up close.

00:02:41.773 --> 00:02:46.794
Next in the lighthouse: guessing the next word, and keeping score.

00:02:47.114 --> 00:02:47.962
Goodnight.

